{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":112467,"num_turns":4,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"77c63d60-0bf4-510b-a4b6-43e40060d64c","total_cost_usd":0.882287,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15704,"cache_read_input_tokens":23059,"output_tokens":10825,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15704,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2945,"cache_read_input_tokens":13787,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1917,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1917},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":3728,"outputTokens":22,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.003838,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-fable-5":{"inputTokens":6,"outputTokens":10825,"cacheReadInputTokens":23059,"cacheCreationInputTokens":15704,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.878449,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer_participant_id\":\"actor:dependence-free-batch-e2-reviewer-fable-26\",\"reviews\":[{\"case_id\":\"case:fa5259eb594c121b4dac\",\"verdict\":\"demonstrated_issue\",\"issue_class\":\"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure\",\"bounded_statement\":\"For the selected result only: the reported rejection of the no-preference null (exact binomial test, 15 of 20 pooled trials on the marked arm, two-sided p = 0.0414, alpha = 0.05) is obtained by entering all four trials from each of the five colonies as 20 independent Bernoulli observations, even though data-description.md declares colony_id as the independent-unit column and states that same-colony tests share nest, forager pool, and extract batch. With only 5 independent units, the pooled test overstates the effective sample size; at the declared unit level 4 of 5 colonies show a marked-arm majority (two-sided sign test p = 0.375), so the significance claim at alpha = 0.05 is not supported by an analysis respecting the declared independent unit. This is bounded to the supplied files and the selected result; it does not claim the underlying biological effect is absent.\",\"root_cause\":\"workflow/analysis.py performs no per-colony aggregation or clustering adjustment: summarise() counts successes across all 20 rows and main() passes the pooled counts directly to scipy.stats.binomtest(n_marked, n_trials, 0.5), whose exact null distribution treats each of the n_trials rows as an independent observation. Because each colony_id contributes four rows, each authorized independent unit enters the row-independent binomial procedure four times, inflating the effective sample size from 5 to 20 and producing the p = 0.0414 rejection reported in results/report.md.\",\"evidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The data description names colony_id as the independent-unit column and states each colony appears on four rows.\",\"observed_value\":\"independent unit = colony_id; 4 rows per colony\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":13,\"end_line\":13,\"quoted_text\":\"Independent unit column: colony_id\"},{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":18,\"end_line\":19,\"quoted_text\":\"- colony_id: the source colony (COL-01 through COL-05); each colony contributed\\n  four tests, so the same colony appears on four rows\"}]},{\"description\":\"The description explicitly flags within-colony dependence: colonies differ noticeably and same-colony tests share nest, forager pool, and extract batch.\",\"observed_value\":\"declared between-colony heterogeneity and shared within-colony conditions\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":28,\"end_line\":31,\"quoted_text\":\"There are 20 rows in total: 5 colonies x 4 tests. Colonies differ noticeably from\\none another in how strongly they follow the extract, and tests from the same\\ncolony were run with the same nest, the same forager pool, and the same batch of\\npheromone extract.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The workflow pools all rows across colonies and feeds the pooled counts to an exact binomial test that assumes independent trials, with no use of colony_id anywhere in the analysis.\",\"observed_value\":\"binomtest(n_marked=15, n_trials=20, p=0.5)\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":27,\"end_line\":28,\"quoted_text\":\"    n_trials = len(trials)\\n    n_marked = sum(1 for row in trials if row[\\\"arm_chosen\\\"] == MARKED_ARM)\"},{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":75,\"end_line\":75,\"quoted_text\":\"    outcome = binomtest(n_marked, n_trials, NULL_P, alternative=\\\"two-sided\\\")\"}]},{\"description\":\"The report states the selected result: 20 pooled trials, 15 marked, two-sided p = 0.0414, null rejected at alpha = 0.05.\",\"observed_value\":{\"n_trials\":20,\"n_marked\":15,\"p_value\":0.0414,\"alpha\":0.05},\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":7,\"end_line\":8,\"quoted_text\":\"All 20 trials were pooled and submitted to an exact binomial test\\n(`scipy.stats.binomtest`, two-sided) against the no-preference null p = 0.500.\"},{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":17,\"end_line\":17,\"quoted_text\":\"[selected-result] Exact binomial test: 15 of 20 trials took the marked arm (proportion 0.750) against a null proportion of 0.500; two-sided p = 0.0414, so the no-preference null is rejected at alpha = 0.05.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The raw data show colony-clustered outcomes (per-colony marked counts of 4/4, 4/4, 3/4, 3/4, 1/4): COL-01 and COL-02 are uniformly marked while COL-05 is majority plain, corroborating within-colony dependence.\",\"observed_value\":{\"COL-01\":\"4/4 marked\",\"COL-02\":\"4/4 marked\",\"COL-03\":\"3/4 marked\",\"COL-04\":\"3/4 marked\",\"COL-05\":\"1/4 marked\"},\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":2,\"end_line\":5,\"quoted_text\":\"COL-01,1,marked,41.2,14\\nCOL-01,2,marked,55.8,11\\nCOL-01,3,marked,63.5,17\\nCOL-01,4,marked,79.5,9\"},{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":18,\"end_line\":21,\"quoted_text\":\"COL-05,1,plain,53.2,10\\nCOL-05,2,plain,60.8,6\\nCOL-05,3,marked,62.5,13\\nCOL-05,4,plain,63.5,9\"}]}],\"counterevidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The data description also defines one trial as one row, and each row records a genuinely distinct release of a forager, so the pooling is not literal duplication of records; the report is also fully transparent that all 20 trials were pooled. This mitigates concerns of fabrication or hidden methodology but does not restore the binomial test's independence assumption across repeated entries of the same declared independent unit.\",\"observed_value\":\"one trial = one row; pooling openly disclosed\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":14,\"end_line\":14,\"quoted_text\":\"One trial is: one row\"},{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":5,\"end_line\":8,\"quoted_text\":\"Each row of `data/input.csv` is one Y-bridge choice trial. A trial counted\\nas a success when the focal forager committed to the pheromone-marked arm.\\nAll 20 trials were pooled and submitted to an exact binomial test\\n(`scipy.stats.binomtest`, two-sided) against the no-preference null p = 0.500.\"}]}],\"falsification_attempt\":\"I attempted to falsify the finding three ways. (1) Recomputed the pooled statistic from the raw CSV: 15 of 20 rows are marked, and the exact two-sided binomial p is 2 x 21700/2^20 = 0.0414, confirming the code and report are internally consistent, so the issue is not a computation error. (2) Re-ran the inference at the declared independent-unit level: per-colony marked counts are 4/4, 4/4, 3/4, 3/4, 1/4, so 4 of 5 colonies show a marked majority; a two-sided sign test on 5 colonies gives p = 2 x 6/32 = 0.375, so the alpha = 0.05 rejection disappears when each colony enters the procedure once — the significance is therefore attributable to the repeated per-colony entries rather than robust to them. (3) Searched workflow/analysis.py for any use of colony_id, per-colony aggregation, or clustering/mixed-model adjustment that could justify row-level pooling; there is none — colony_id is read but never referenced in the analysis. All three checks left the issue standing.\",\"cross_case_evidence_used\":false,\"unresolved_material_questions\":[],\"self_reported_confidence\":\"high\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-e2-reviewer-fable-26","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:fa5259eb594c121b4dac","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"For the selected result only: the reported rejection of the no-preference null (exact binomial test, 15 of 20 pooled trials on the marked arm, two-sided p = 0.0414, alpha = 0.05) is obtained by entering all four trials from each of the five colonies as 20 independent Bernoulli observations, even though data-description.md declares colony_id as the independent-unit column and states that same-colony tests share nest, forager pool, and extract batch. With only 5 independent units, the pooled test overstates the effective sample size; at the declared unit level 4 of 5 colonies show a marked-arm majority (two-sided sign test p = 0.375), so the significance claim at alpha = 0.05 is not supported by an analysis respecting the declared independent unit. This is bounded to the supplied files and the selected result; it does not claim the underlying biological effect is absent.","root_cause":"workflow/analysis.py performs no per-colony aggregation or clustering adjustment: summarise() counts successes across all 20 rows and main() passes the pooled counts directly to scipy.stats.binomtest(n_marked, n_trials, 0.5), whose exact null distribution treats each of the n_trials rows as an independent observation. Because each colony_id contributes four rows, each authorized independent unit enters the row-independent binomial procedure four times, inflating the effective sample size from 5 to 20 and producing the p = 0.0414 rejection reported in results/report.md.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description names colony_id as the independent-unit column and states each colony appears on four rows.","observed_value":"independent unit = colony_id; 4 rows per colony","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":13,"end_line":13,"quoted_text":"Independent unit column: colony_id"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":18,"end_line":19,"quoted_text":"- colony_id: the source colony (COL-01 through COL-05); each colony contributed\n  four tests, so the same colony appears on four rows"}]},{"description":"The description explicitly flags within-colony dependence: colonies differ noticeably and same-colony tests share nest, forager pool, and extract batch.","observed_value":"declared between-colony heterogeneity and shared within-colony conditions","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":28,"end_line":31,"quoted_text":"There are 20 rows in total: 5 colonies x 4 tests. Colonies differ noticeably from\none another in how strongly they follow the extract, and tests from the same\ncolony were run with the same nest, the same forager pool, and the same batch of\npheromone extract."}]},{"description":"The workflow pools all rows across colonies and feeds the pooled counts to an exact binomial test that assumes independent trials, with no use of colony_id anywhere in the analysis.","observed_value":"binomtest(n_marked=15, n_trials=20, p=0.5)","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":27,"end_line":28,"quoted_text":"    n_trials = len(trials)\n    n_marked = sum(1 for row in trials if row[\"arm_chosen\"] == MARKED_ARM)"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":75,"end_line":75,"quoted_text":"    outcome = binomtest(n_marked, n_trials, NULL_P, alternative=\"two-sided\")"}]},{"description":"The report states the selected result: 20 pooled trials, 15 marked, two-sided p = 0.0414, null rejected at alpha = 0.05.","observed_value":{"n_trials":20,"n_marked":15,"p_value":0.0414,"alpha":0.05},"source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":7,"end_line":8,"quoted_text":"All 20 trials were pooled and submitted to an exact binomial test\n(`scipy.stats.binomtest`, two-sided) against the no-preference null p = 0.500."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":17,"end_line":17,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Exact binomial test: 15 of 20 trials took the marked arm (proportion 0.750) against a null proportion of 0.500; two-sided p = 0.0414, so the no-preference null is rejected at alpha = 0.05."}]},{"description":"The raw data show colony-clustered outcomes (per-colony marked counts of 4/4, 4/4, 3/4, 3/4, 1/4): COL-01 and COL-02 are uniformly marked while COL-05 is majority plain, corroborating within-colony dependence.","observed_value":{"COL-01":"4/4 marked","COL-02":"4/4 marked","COL-03":"3/4 marked","COL-04":"3/4 marked","COL-05":"1/4 marked"},"source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"COL-01,1,marked,41.2,14\nCOL-01,2,marked,55.8,11\nCOL-01,3,marked,63.5,17\nCOL-01,4,marked,79.5,9"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":18,"end_line":21,"quoted_text":"COL-05,1,plain,53.2,10\nCOL-05,2,plain,60.8,6\nCOL-05,3,marked,62.5,13\nCOL-05,4,plain,63.5,9"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description also defines one trial as one row, and each row records a genuinely distinct release of a forager, so the pooling is not literal duplication of records; the report is also fully transparent that all 20 trials were pooled. This mitigates concerns of fabrication or hidden methodology but does not restore the binomial test's independence assumption across repeated entries of the same declared independent unit.","observed_value":"one trial = one row; pooling openly disclosed","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":14,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"One trial is: one row"},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":5,"end_line":8,"quoted_text":"Each row of `data/input.csv` is one Y-bridge choice trial. A trial counted\nas a success when the focal forager committed to the pheromone-marked arm.\nAll 20 trials were pooled and submitted to an exact binomial test\n(`scipy.stats.binomtest`, two-sided) against the no-preference null p = 0.500."}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to falsify the finding three ways. (1) Recomputed the pooled statistic from the raw CSV: 15 of 20 rows are marked, and the exact two-sided binomial p is 2 x 21700/2^20 = 0.0414, confirming the code and report are internally consistent, so the issue is not a computation error. (2) Re-ran the inference at the declared independent-unit level: per-colony marked counts are 4/4, 4/4, 3/4, 3/4, 1/4, so 4 of 5 colonies show a marked majority; a two-sided sign test on 5 colonies gives p = 2 x 6/32 = 0.375, so the alpha = 0.05 rejection disappears when each colony enters the procedure once — the significance is therefore attributable to the repeated per-colony entries rather than robust to them. (3) Searched workflow/analysis.py for any use of colony_id, per-colony aggregation, or clustering/mixed-model adjustment that could justify row-level pooling; there is none — colony_id is read but never referenced in the analysis. All three checks left the issue standing.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]},"ttft_ms":39290,"ttft_stream_ms":1646,"time_to_request_ms":20,"type":"result","duration_ms":112140,"uuid":"987b49dc-4af7-488e-8c24-316fbccc33bc"}
